================================================================================
  PART 3 — COLOR FIX (TPM408 quirk)
================================================================================

3.1  Symptom
------------
Colors came out in the wrong order despite correct wiring:
    Sent RED   -> saw BLUE
    Sent BLUE  -> saw RED
    Sent GREEN -> saw PURPLE (composite of R+B leak)

The TPM408's ST7789 ships configured as BGR (blue-green-red byte order),
while TFT_eSPI's default assumption is RGB. Result: every pixel's red and
blue channels are swapped.

3.2  Attempted fixes that DID NOT work
--------------------------------------
a) Build flags in platformio.ini:
       -D TFT_RGB_ORDER=TFT_BGR
       -D TFT_INVERSION_ON
   Why it failed:
       TFT_eSPI's User_Setup.h and build flags have ordering dependencies.
       With USER_SETUP_LOADED=1, some flags are silently ignored by the
       library's macro logic. The Serial diagnostic showed
       TFT_RGB_ORDER = 0 (RGB) despite the flag being set — proving the
       flag never reached the compiler.

b) Runtime MADCTL writes in setup():
       tft.writecommand(0x36);
       tft.writedata(0xA8);
   Why it failed:
       TFT_eSPI's writecommand/writedata must be called inside an active
       SPI transaction (startWrite/endWrite). Called outside, they are
       silently dropped or overwritten by the library's own display
       updates. Runtime changes had no effect — confirmed by cycling
       through 8 MADCTL values and seeing zero visual change.

3.3  The fix that WORKED
------------------------
Two edits in lib/TFT_eSPI/TFT_Drivers/ST7789_Init.h:

Edit 1 — MADCTL register write (line ~19):
    Before:
        writedata(TFT_MAD_COLOR_ORDER);
    After:
        writedata(0x68);   // MX | MV | BGR

    Bit breakdown of 0x68:
        0x20 = MV   (row/column exchange — needed for landscape)
        0x40 = MX   (mirror X — compensates for panel mounting)
        0x08 = BGR  (blue-green-red color order)
        Result: correct landscape orientation + correct colors.

Edit 2 — Inversion command (line ~102):
    Before:
        writecommand(ST7789_INVON);
    After:
        // writecommand(ST7789_INVON);

    Why:
        Forced inversion was making white appear as black and turning
        every color into its complement. Commenting it out leaves the
        panel in normal (non-inverted) mode, which is correct for this
        TPM408 batch.

3.4  Why these edits work but the flags don't
---------------------------------------------
TFT_eSPI's init sequence is a compile-time table. It runs once, at
tft.init(), before any user code. Whatever values are in that table are
what the panel sees for the entire session. Runtime writes after init
either get ignored or overwritten.

So the only reliable way to change color order or inversion is to edit
the init table directly.

3.5  Also fixed in lib/TFT_eSPI/User_Setup.h
--------------------------------------------
    Before:
        #define TFT_RGB_ORDER TFT_RGB
    After:
        // #define TFT_RGB_ORDER TFT_RGB
        // #define TFT_RGB_ORDER TFT_BGR   (both commented)

    Before:
        #define TFT_INVERSION_ON
    After:
        // #define TFT_INVERSION_ON

Why:
    These macros conflict with the hardcoded 0x68 in ST7789_Init.h. If
    the library uses TFT_RGB_ORDER in some code paths and the hardcoded
    0x68 in others, the result is nondeterministic — colors change
    between runs. Removing the macros leaves the init table as the
    single source of truth.

